    {{TARGET_AUDIENCE_BLOCK}}



{{STRUCTURE_BLOCK}}
{{STRUCTURE_USAGE_GUIDANCE}}




    Replace concise verbs with slightly longer action process descriptions.
    Example: "Manage" → "Conduct management work"; "Configure" → "Perform configuration"; "Implement" → "Succeed in implementing."


    Appropriately add auxiliary connectors to make sentences fuller.


    Use/Employ → Utilize/Adopt; Based on → In light of; Through/Using → By means of/Relying on; Reason → Cause/Rationale; Matches → Aligns with; Immediately → Promptly.


    Integrate explanatory information inside parentheses into the sentence using guides like "namely", "that is", or "for example".
    Example: ORM (Object-Relational Mapping) → Object-Relational Mapping, namely ORM.


    Appropriately shift formal conditional clauses into slightly more conversational forms (e.g., if → assuming/in case).





  All technical terms, code snippets, configuration items, and API paths MUST remain completely unaltered.
  Add accessible explanations for complex technical concepts without altering the original meaning.
  The rewritten text MUST maintain exactly the same technical logic and causal relationships as the original.
  The final word count should roughly match the original, avoiding excessive padding.
  Naturally weave parenthetical explanations into the main sentence to reduce brackets.
  Use sentence adjustment techniques from the rewriting rules to avoid structural monotony.
  {{REFERENCE_MATERIAL_STRATEGY}}
  {{INTERNAL_LINKING_STRATEGY}}




  Technical terms, code snippets, library names, config items, API paths, version numbers, etc. MUST exactly match the original text. This is the absolute highest priority.
  Rewritten sentences must express the exact same technical logic, causal links, and functional descriptions as the original.
  The entire article must be written in {{LANGUAGE_NAME}}.
  Output length should basically match the reference material, adjusted naturally through rewriting rules.
  The use of "I," "We," "I think" and similar first-person pronoun expressions is prohibited.
  Avoid overly colloquial filler phrases.
  Prohibit clichés like "In summary," "It is worth noting," or "As is well known."
  {{REFERENCE_MATERIAL_PRINCIPLE}}
  {{INTERNAL_LINKING_STANDARD}}



  Maintain high perplexity and burstiness. In technical articles, appropriately mix short sentences (to define points) with long sentences (to expand explanations) to avoid a statistically smooth distribution of sentence lengths.
  Occasionally include minor grammar quirks or natural imperfections, such as slight punctuation variations or slightly wordy but more natural technical explanations, simulating a real engineer rewriting documentation.
  Avoid using colons, semicolons, and dashes (typical traits of AI tech docs). Change technical colon-led lists to use "including", replace semicolons with commas/conjunctions, and break up dash sentences with periods.




  Use standard markdown format to output the pure article body.
  Strictly limit the output to the article body itself, starting from the first markdown heading (##) to the end of the last natural paragraph. Do not include any conversational, explanatory, or descriptive text outside the content.
